---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Sidebar from '../../components/Sidebar.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro';
import { frameworkNav } from '../../lib/sections';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Trust model"
  description="Trust in UniDPP is SIGNATIF-shaped: jurisdictional threshold roots, trust lists, a multi-witnessed master list, transparency logs, algorithm agility, and coverage-report-based verification — with Confium quorum ceremonies."
  eyebrow="Framework — trust"
  sectioned
>
  <Sidebar slot="rail" heading="Framework" items={frameworkNav} />
  <h1>Trust model</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Trust in UniDPP is SIGNATIF-shaped, not a single global PKI: jurisdictional trust
    authorities operate as threshold groups, jurisdictional trust lists feed a globally
    multi-witnessed master list, transparency logs anchor both trust events and passport
    events, and verification returns graded verdicts with coverage reports rather than
    booleans.
  </p>

  <section id="registry" class="plate" aria-labelledby="registry-h">
    <h2 id="registry-h">The trust registry</h2>
    <p>
      The trust layer is a registry of the same discipline as the semantic layer: the FERIN
      registry (ISO 19135) and the SIGNATIF trust registry are siblings. Its elements:
      jurisdictional trust authorities as threshold groups — no single key, no single
      country; jurisdictional trust lists; a globally multi-witnessed master list
      maintained by an M-of-K quorum of independent logs; transparency logs anchoring both
      trust events and passport events; and an algorithm-agility registry governing
      cryptographic-suite transitions, including the post-quantum schedule.
    </p>
    <DataTable
      columns={['Lineage', 'Inherited mechanism']}
      rows={[
        ['WebPKI + Certificate Transparency', 'Transparency logging of anchored artifacts.'],
        ['ePassport CSCA/PKD (ICAO)', 'Governmental master lists of trust anchors.'],
        ['Code signing', 'Key protection and timestamping.'],
        ['eIDAS', 'Accreditation of trust-service providers.'],
      ]}
      caption="Precedent systems unified under SIGNATIF’s threshold quorum, four-layer scope enforcement, and algorithm agility."
    />
    <p>
      <a href="https://signatif.org">SIGNATIF</a> is published; Confium provides the
      threshold quorum operations for root and master-list ceremonies in
      <code>unidpp-signatif</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="co-signatures" aria-labelledby="cosig-h">
    <h2 id="cosig-h">Co-signatures and profile-bound suites</h2>
    <p>
      Jurisdictional cryptographic suites are part of the profile, not the platform. The CN
      profile binds SM2/SM3/SM4/SM9 and TLCP — all published as ISO/IEC mechanisms
      (14888-3, 10118-3, 18033-3); the US federal profile binds FIPS suites; the EU
      profile binds eIDAS-acceptable suites. The same payload carries multiple signatures
      side by side (for example ECDSA + SM2 + ML-DSA); each verifier checks only what its
      profile-scoped acceptance policy requires. Envelope encryption wraps payload keys per
      recipient under each jurisdiction’s permitted key-encapsulation mechanism, and
      migration phases — composite, then post-quantum — proceed per jurisdiction under the
      agility registry. CNML is the post-quantum ESDC realization.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="revocation" class="plate" aria-labelledby="revocation-h">
    <h2 id="revocation-h">Revocation and distrust</h2>
    <p>
      Revocation is not one operation: <strong>the reason determines
      retroactivity</strong>. Prospective reasons (key compromise after a point in time,
      cessation, supersession) leave earlier verifications valid, protected by
      timestamping. Retroactive reasons — <span class="neg">misissuance, fraudulent
      issuance, authority compromised during a window</span> — void validity ab initio or
      from the window start, exactly as browser root-store distrust of a misissuing
      certificate authority invalidates its timestamped certificates.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because law needs more than one answer, verdicts state which of three readings they
      answer: <strong>evidentiary</strong> (what could a diligent verifier know at T, given
      the then-current trust state — this protects good-faith actors, whose stamps are the
      proof of diligence), <strong>current-state</strong> (fraud voids ab initio,
      preventing laundering of fraudulent goods), and <strong>cryptographic</strong>
      (signature and chain validity alone).
    </p>
    <p>
      Declaring a passport fraudulent is a graph event, not only a trust-list event: it
      traverses the provenance DAG downstream — the same machinery as recalls, with a
      different cause — tainting derived passports and recomputing affected claims (a
      recycled-content claim built on stolen material collapses). Mass-balance accounting
      carries taint explicitly, and custodians are notified.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="stamps" aria-labelledby="stamps-h">
    <h2 id="stamps-h">Stamps</h2>
    <p>
      A stamp is a lens-scoped attestation: the attester’s signature over the subject, its
      state commitment, the lens ID and version, the mode (live or snapshot), the verdict
      with its coverage report, and log-anchored time. Stamps accrete append-only and are
      valid under the stamper’s lens regardless of the issuer’s. Stamps may carry quantity
      context (“verified 500&nbsp;g of A at T”), inherited by downstream transformations.
      In the travel analogy the framework completes: visas are jurisdiction stamps in the
      passport; border guards are verifiers; and the passport itself remains the property
      of the traveller, not the state that inspected it.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
